  "correct_answer": "4028",
  "formal_question": "The diagram shows a shape made from six squares, each measuring $1 \\mathrm{~cm}$ by $1 \\mathrm{~cm}$. The shape has perimeter of length $14 \\mathrm{~cm}$. The zigzag shape is then continued until it has 2013 squares. What is the length, in $\\mathrm{cm}$, of the perimeter of the new shape?",
  "material_difference": "Old Plane428 screened with no saved design/calls: styling seams alone would not specify a false recurrence. New two concrete middle-square examples each link one consumed incoming contact (blue) to two persistent exterior flanks (red). This models true net growth+2 while tempting multiplication by n without the initial+2 cap constant. No endpoint is hidden, weakened or recolored, and the given six-square perimeter14 is unchanged.",
  "primary_unique_math_proof": "All6 squares form a path of5 edge adjacencies, verified from exact native shared-edge sets; no other positive-length contacts exist. P6=4*6-2*5=14, matching the text. Extending the same zigzag adds one square sharing exactly one full side, hence deltaP=4-2=2. P2013=14+2*(2013-6)=4028, equivalently Pn=2n+2. The false answer4026 counts only2n persistent flank units and omits the two unchanged terminal cap units. The two colored examples are squares2 and4, with predecessor/successor shown; each blue incoming contact is internal in the final figure, each red flank external. Both endcaps remain full black.",
  "human_semantics": "Numerical perimeter under continuation. Existing open edge styles are growth examples, not extra squares, cuts or altered boundary ownership. Blue shared contacts remain physically internal with two cells incident; the black complete silhouette and given14 make the target unambiguous.",
